<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>

<worksheet xml:id="ma310-written-hw02">
<title>MA-310 Calculus III: Written Homework 2</title>
  <!-- ====================================================== -->
  <!-- Question 1 -->
  <!-- ====================================================== -->

  <page xml:id="page-question-1">

    <exercise xml:id="question-1">
      <statement>
        <p>
          Let
          <me>
            \mathbf{a}=3\mathbf{i}-\mathbf{j}-4\mathbf{k}
          </me>
          and
          <me>
            \mathbf{b}=2\mathbf{i}+5\mathbf{j}-2\mathbf{k}.
          </me>
          Find the following:
        </p>

        <ol>
          <li>
            <p>The dot product of <m>\mathbf{a}</m> with itself,
              <me>\mathbf{a}\mathbin{\boldsymbol{\cdot}}\mathbf{a}.</me>
            </p>
          </li>

          <li>
            <p>The dot product of <m>\mathbf{a}</m> and <m>\mathbf{b}</m>,
              <me>\mathbf{a}\mathbin{\boldsymbol{\cdot}}\mathbf{b}.</me>
            </p>
          </li>

          <li>
            <p>The cross product of <m>\mathbf{b}</m> and <m>\mathbf{a}</m>,
              <me>\mathbf{b}\mathbin{\boldsymbol{\times}}\mathbf{a}.</me>
            </p>
          </li>
        </ol>
      </statement>

      <solution>
        <ol>
          <li>
            <p>For the dot product of <m>\mathbf{a}</m> with itself:</p>

            <me>
              \mathbf{a}\mathbin{\boldsymbol{\cdot}}\mathbf{a}
              =(3)(3)+(-1)(-1)+(-4)(-4)=9+1+16=26.
            </me>

            <p>
              Therefore,
              <me>
                \mathbf{a}\mathbin{\boldsymbol{\cdot}}\mathbf{a}=26.
              </me>
            </p>
          </li>

          <li>
            <p>For the dot product of <m>\mathbf{a}</m> and <m>\mathbf{b}</m>:</p>

            <me>
              \mathbf{a}\mathbin{\boldsymbol{\cdot}}\mathbf{b}
              =(3)(2)+(-1)(5)+(-4)(-2)=6-5+8=9.
            </me>

            <p>
              Therefore,
              <me>
                \mathbf{a}\mathbin{\boldsymbol{\cdot}}\mathbf{b}=9.
              </me>
            </p>
          </li>

          <li>
            <p>
              For the cross product, write the vectors in component form:
            </p>

            <me>
              \mathbf{b}=\langle 2,5,-2\rangle,
              \qquad
              \mathbf{a}=\langle 3,-1,-4\rangle.
            </me>

            <me>
              \mathbf{b}\mathbin{\boldsymbol{\times}}\mathbf{a}
              =\left\langle
              5(-4)-(-2)(-1),
              -\left(2(-4)-(-2)(3)\right),
              2(-1)-5(3)\right\rangle
              =\langle-22,2,-17\rangle.
            </me>

            <p>
              Therefore,
              <me>
                \mathbf{b}\mathbin{\boldsymbol{\times}}\mathbf{a}
                =\langle-22,2,-17\rangle.
              </me>
            </p>
          </li>
        </ol>
      </solution>
    </exercise>

  </page>


  <!-- ====================================================== -->
  <!-- Question 2 -->
  <!-- ====================================================== -->

  <page xml:id="page-question-2">

    <exercise xml:id="question-2">
      <statement>
        <p>
          Find an equation of the plane that passes through the point
          <me>(-1,6,0)</me>
          and is perpendicular to the line
          <me>
            x=-1+t,\qquad y=6-2t,\qquad z=3t.
          </me>
        </p>
      </statement>

      <solution>
        <p>
          The direction vector of the line is
        </p>

        <me>
          \mathbf{n}=\langle1,-2,3\rangle.
        </me>

        <p>
          Since the plane is perpendicular to the line, this direction
          vector is a normal vector to the plane.
        </p>

        <p>
          Using the point-normal form of a plane,
        </p>

        <me>
          \mathbf{n}\mathbin{\boldsymbol{\cdot}}
          \langle x-x_0,y-y_0,z-z_0\rangle=0,
        </me>

        <p>we obtain</p>

        <me>
          \langle1,-2,3\rangle
          \mathbin{\boldsymbol{\cdot}}
          \langle x+1,y-6,z\rangle=0.
        </me>

        <me>
          x+1-2(y-6)+3z=0,
          \qquad x-2y+3z=-13.
        </me>

        <p>
          Therefore, an equation of the plane is
        </p>

        <me>
          \boxed{x-2y+3z=-13}.
        </me>
      </solution>
    </exercise>

  </page>


  <!-- ====================================================== -->
  <!-- Question 3 -->
  <!-- ====================================================== -->

  <page xml:id="page-question-3">

    <exercise xml:id="question-3">
      <statement>
        <p>
          The lines <m>L_1</m> and <m>L_2</m> have the following
          parametric equations for <m>t\in\mathbb{R}</m>:
        </p>

        <me>
          L_1: x=1+2t,\qquad y=3-4t,\qquad z=-2+t,
          \qquad
          L_2: x=-5-t,\qquad y=2-3t,\qquad z=4+3t.
        </me>

        <p>
          Find the angle between <m>L_1</m> and <m>L_2</m>.
        </p>
      </statement>

      <solution>
        <p>
          The direction vectors of the two lines are
        </p>

        <me>
          \mathbf{u}=\langle2,-4,1\rangle,
          \qquad
          \mathbf{v}=\langle-1,-3,3\rangle.
        </me>

        <p>
          Use the dot-product formula
        </p>

        <me>
          \cos\theta=
          \frac{\mathbf{u}\mathbin{\boldsymbol{\cdot}}\mathbf{v}}
          {|\mathbf{u}||\mathbf{v}|}.
        </me>

        <me>
          \mathbf{u}\mathbin{\boldsymbol{\cdot}}\mathbf{v}
          =(2)(-1)+(-4)(-3)+(1)(3)=-2+12+3=13.
        </me>

        <p>
          The magnitudes are
        </p>

        <me>
          |\mathbf{u}|=\sqrt{2^2+(-4)^2+1^2}=\sqrt{21},
          \qquad
          |\mathbf{v}|=\sqrt{(-1)^2+(-3)^2+3^2}=\sqrt{19}.
        </me>

        <p>
          Therefore,
        </p>

        <me>
          \cos\theta=\frac{13}{\sqrt{21}\sqrt{19}}
          =\frac{13}{\sqrt{399}}.
        </me>

        <me>
          \theta=\arccos\left(\frac{13}{\sqrt{399}}\right)
          \approx49.4^\circ.
        </me>

        <p>
          Therefore, the angle between the two lines is
          <me>\boxed{\theta\approx49.4^\circ}.</me>
        </p>
      </solution>
    </exercise>

  </page>


  <!-- ====================================================== -->
  <!-- Question 4 -->
  <!-- ====================================================== -->

  <page xml:id="page-question-4">

    <exercise xml:id="question-4">
      <statement>
        <p>
          Find all real numbers <m>t</m> for which the vectors
        </p>

        <me>
          \mathbf{u}=\langle2t,4,3\rangle
          \qquad\text{and}\qquad
          \mathbf{v}=\langle-t,t,1\rangle
        </me>

        <p>are orthogonal.</p>
      </statement>

      <solution>
        <p>
          Two vectors are orthogonal when their dot product is zero.
          Therefore,
        </p>

        <me>
          \mathbf{u}\mathbin{\boldsymbol{\cdot}}\mathbf{v}=0.
        </me>

        <me>
          (2t)(-t)+(4)(t)+(3)(1)=0,
          \qquad -2t^2+4t+3=0.
        </me>

        <p>
          Multiply by <m>-1</m>:
        </p>

        <me>
          2t^2-4t-3=0.
        </me>

        <p>
          Using the quadratic formula,
        </p>

        <me>
          t=
          \frac{-(-4)\mathbin{\boldsymbol{\pm}}
          \sqrt{(-4)^2-4(2)(-3)}}{2(2)}.
        </me>

        <me>
          t=\frac{4\mathbin{\boldsymbol{\pm}}\sqrt{40}}{4}
          =1\mathbin{\boldsymbol{\pm}}\frac{\sqrt{10}}{2}.
        </me>

        <p>
          Therefore,
        </p>

        <me>
          \boxed{t=1+\frac{\sqrt{10}}{2}
          \quad\text{or}\quad
          t=1-\frac{\sqrt{10}}{2}}.
        </me>
      </solution>
    </exercise>

  </page>


  <!-- ====================================================== -->
  <!-- Question 5 -->
  <!-- ====================================================== -->

  <page xml:id="page-question-5">

    <exercise xml:id="question-5">
      <statement>
        <p>
          Find two vectors, each with magnitude <m>10</m>, which are
          orthogonal to both
        </p>

        <me>
          \mathbf{u}=\langle-2,3,1\rangle
          \qquad\text{and}\qquad
          \mathbf{v}=\langle4,4,-1\rangle.
        </me>
      </statement>

      <solution>
        <p>
          A vector perpendicular to both <m>\mathbf{u}</m> and
          <m>\mathbf{v}</m> can be found using the cross product:
        </p>

        <me>
  \mathbf{u}\mathbin{\boldsymbol{\times}}\mathbf{v}
  =
  \left\langle
  3(-1)-1(4),
  -\left((-2)(-1)-1(4)\right),
  (-2)(4)-3(4)
  \right\rangle.
</me>

        <me>
          \mathbf{u}\mathbin{\boldsymbol{\times}}\mathbf{v}
          =-7\mathbf{i}+2\mathbf{j}-20\mathbf{k}.
        </me>

        <p>
          Thus,
        </p>

        <me>
          \mathbf{u}\mathbin{\boldsymbol{\times}}\mathbf{v}
          =\langle-7,2,-20\rangle.
        </me>

        <p>
          Its magnitude is
        </p>

        <me>
          |\mathbf{u}\mathbin{\boldsymbol{\times}}\mathbf{v}|
          =\sqrt{(-7)^2+2^2+(-20)^2}
          =\sqrt{49+4+400}=\sqrt{453}.
        </me>

        <p>
          Therefore, a unit vector perpendicular to both vectors is
        </p>

        <me>
          \frac{1}{\sqrt{453}}\langle-7,2,-20\rangle.
        </me>

        <p>
          To obtain magnitude <m>10</m>, multiply by <m>10</m>:
        </p>

        <me>
          \mathbf{w}_1=
          \frac{10}{\sqrt{453}}\langle-7,2,-20\rangle.
        </me>

        <p>
          The vector in the opposite direction also has magnitude
          <m>10</m>:
        </p>

        <me>
          \mathbf{w}_2=
          -\frac{10}{\sqrt{453}}\langle-7,2,-20\rangle.
        </me>

        <p>
          Therefore, the two vectors are
        </p>

        <me>
          \boxed{
          \mathbf{w}_1=
          \frac{10}{\sqrt{453}}\langle-7,2,-20\rangle,
          \qquad
          \mathbf{w}_2=
          -\frac{10}{\sqrt{453}}\langle-7,2,-20\rangle
          }.
        </me>
      </solution>
    </exercise>

  </page>

</worksheet>